# Copy this file outside the repository, set mode 0600, and replace every
# placeholder. `imessage-proxy` refuses a configuration containing any key it
# does not recognise, so the keys below are the whole set: two that must be
# present, and four that have defaults and may be left out entirely.
#
# The installer writes this file for you. Editing it by hand is for changing a
# setting afterwards, and every change applies at the next start.

# Required. The reviewed imsg executable and the digest it is pinned to.
IMESSAGE_PROXY_IMSG_BIN=REPLACE_WITH_ABSOLUTE_IMSG_BINARY
IMESSAGE_PROXY_IMSG_SHA256=REPLACE_WITH_REVIEWED_64_HEX_SHA256

# Optional. The loopback port the one listener answers on. Default 8765.
#IMESSAGE_PROXY_PORT=8765

# Optional. The address to bind. Default 127.0.0.1, which is the only address
# reachable from the Mac alone. Naming another one serves the API over plain
# HTTP to whatever network that address is on, with the bearer key as the only
# control; 0.0.0.0 means every interface, including ones the Mac joins later.
#IMESSAGE_PROXY_BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1

# Optional. One extra browser origin the console may be loaded from, for an
# installation published behind a TLS terminator. The origins the server accepts
# are otherwise derived from what it bound, which is always http:// and always
# an address or localhost, so a console served at a name over https needs that
# name written down here or every send it makes is refused. Scheme, host and an
# optional port, with no path or trailing slash. Empty unless you run one.
#IMESSAGE_PROXY_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://messages.example.com

# Optional. Whether the service may read the Messages database. Default enabled;
# the exact word `disabled` turns reading off and leaves sending working.
#IMESSAGE_PROXY_MESSAGES_READ=enabled
